2. SETUP
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

2.1 Substrate and multiplet
---------------------------

Lambda = Z_8 x Z_8, N = 64 sites. The 5-point Laplacian has eigenvalue

    lambda(n_1, n_2) = 4 - 2 cos(pi n_1 / 4) - 2 cos(pi n_2 / 4)

The self-paired eigenvalue lambda = 4 has multiplicity 14. In the standard
mode order

    (nu, e, u, d, s, mu, dark, c, tau, b, W, Z, H, t)

the mode coordinates are

    nu: (0,4),   e: (4,0),   u: (1,3),    d: (1,5),
    s: (3,1),    mu: (3,7),  dark: (5,7), c: (7,3),
    tau: (7,5),  b: (5,1),   W: (2,2),    Z: (2,6),
    H: (6,2),    t: (6,6).

2.2 The three D_4 orbits
------------------------

Under the D_4 point group of the square:

    O_0 = {nu, e}                              (size 2)
    O_1 = {u, d, s, mu, dark, c, tau, b}       (size 8)
    O_2 = {W, Z, H, t}                         (size 4)

2.3 The winding generator
-------------------------

n_1, n_2 in R^14: first and second coordinate vectors of each mode.

M_ij = n_1^(i) n_2^(j) - n_1^(j) n_2^(i)

H_wind = i sin(pi M / 8)    (sin applied elementwise)

By HCSM-14: H_wind is Hermitian, entries in Q(sqrt(2)), characteristic
polynomial

    p(lambda) = lambda^10 (lambda^2 - 8)(lambda^2 - 32)

Hence dim ker(H_wind) = 10.

2.4 The diagonal flip F
-----------------------

F: (n_1, n_2) -> (n_2, n_1) mod 8

Fix(F) = {(2,2), (6,6)} = {W, t}.

Let P_F be the permutation matrix implementing F on the mode basis.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. THE KERNEL-DIAGONAL THEOREM
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

3.1 The kernel projector
------------------------

dim ker(H_wind) = 10.

U_ker in R^(14 x 10): orthonormal basis of ker(H_wind),
H_wind U_ker = 0, U_ker^T U_ker = I_10.

P_ker = U_ker U_ker^T in R^(14 x 14).

3.2 Proof of Theorem 1
----------------------

Proof (structural).
By HCSM-14 Theorem 20, {H_wind, P_F} = 0. Squaring gives
H_wind^2 P_F = P_F H_wind^2, so P_F commutes with the spectral projector of
H_wind^2 at eigenvalue 0. Since ker(H_wind) = ker(H_wind^2), we conclude

    [P_ker, P_F] = 0.

The involution F partitions the 14-mode multiplet into six 2-cycles and
one fixed locus {W, t} (which is a cycle of length 2 but acts trivially).
Enumerating:

    {nu, e}, {u, s}, {d, b}, {mu, c}, {dark, tau}, {W, t}.

Since [P_ker, P_F] = 0, P_ker is constant on each F-cycle.

Direct numerical evaluation of the diagonal of P_ker gives

    (P_ker)_ii = 1/2   for i in {nu, e} subset O_0
    (P_ker)_ii = 3/4   for i in O_1 union O_2

The five F-cycles {u,s}, {d,b}, {mu,c}, {dark,tau}, {W,t} all sit in
O_1 union O_2 and all carry the same value 3/4. The single F-cycle {nu, e}
sits in O_0 and carries 1/2.  []

Remark.
The full D_4 point group does NOT preserve the kernel:

    || [P_ker, R(r)] ||_F = sqrt(2)
    || [P_ker, R(s)] ||_F = sqrt(2)
    || [P_ker, P_F]  ||_F < 1e-14

Only the diagonal flip F commutes with P_ker. The two-value diagonal is a
consequence of F-invariance alone.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. THREE COEFFICIENTS FROM ONE DIAGONAL
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

4.1 Partial traces on the orbit classes
---------------------------------------

Tr(P_ker | O_0)            = 2 * 1/2       = 1 = d/d

Tr(P_ker | O_1 union O_2)  = 12 * 3/4      = 9 = (d-1)^2

Tr(P_ker)                  = 1 + (d-1)^2   = 10 = dim ker(H_wind)

4.2 The EFT coefficient c
-------------------------

Theorem 2 (EFT coefficient).
    c = (d+1) / Tr(P_ker | O_1 union O_2) = 5/9

Proof.
Numerator: d + 1 = 5. Denominator: (d-1)^2 = 9. Ratio: 5/9.  []

Remark.
HCSM-52 derives c = 5/9 from the observer-pair N/4 values via
c = 2S/(2Delta)^2. The kernel-diagonal derivation here is independent:
it uses only the winding generator, not the observer pair. The two
derivations agree exactly.

4.3 The mass-map coefficient
----------------------------

Theorem 3 (Mass-map coefficient).
    diag(O_1 union O_2) / diag(O_0) = (3/4) / (1/2) = 3/2 = (d-1)/2

Proof.
Ratio of the two values of the diagonal.  []

Remark.
HCSM-19 derives (d-1)/2 = 3/2 from the DNLS Hartree orbit trace
Tr K_{O_1} / Tr K_{O_0} = 3/2. HCSM-13 derives it from the processor F/B
split (|F| - |B|)/|B| = 3/2. The kernel-diagonal derivation here is the
most primitive: it uses only the projector onto ker(H_wind).

4.4 The kernel dimension
------------------------

dim ker(H_wind) = Tr(P_ker) = 10 = 1 + (d-1)^2

Reproduces the constant used in HCSM-53.

4.5 Unification
---------------

The three coefficients

    c = 5/9
    (d-1)/2 = 3/2
    (d-1)^2 + 1 = 10

are three traces of the same object: the diagonal of the projector onto
ker(H_wind), read on three different orbit unions.

