6. THE KERNEL-T DECOMPOSITION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Theorem 5 (Kernel splitting under the processor).
Let T = I - P_C be the processor of HCSM-13, with image F-sector (rank 10)
and kernel C = {dark, W, Z, H} (rank 4). Then

    dim ( ker(H_wind) intersect im(T) )  = 6 = |F| - |B|
    dim ( ker(H_wind) intersect ker(T) ) = 4 = |B|

The two dimensions sum to 10 = dim ker(H_wind).

Proof (sketch).
Form the stacked matrix [U_ker | U_T] where U_T in R^(14 x 10) is an
orthonormal basis of im(T). Computing the rank of this matrix (or
equivalently of [U_ker | U_C] with U_C a basis of ker(T)) gives the stated
dimensions. Both are verified at machine precision in the companion
script.  []

Remark.
The decomposition ker(H_wind) = (6-dim) + (4-dim) under the processor T
mirrors the F/B split of HCSM-13. The kernel of the winding generator is
not the same as the kernel of the processor; it intersects the bosonic
sector B in 4 dimensions and the fermionic sector F in 6 dimensions.

